    "items": [
      {
        "id": "def-td-rectangular-local-equilibrium-fields",
        "kind": "definition",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "Rectangular local-equilibrium field data",
        "reference": "items/def-td-rectangular-local-equilibrium-fields.md#definition",
        "design_state": "Complete draft carrier authored; scoped checks and independent review remain pending.",
        "dependency_level": 1,
        "deps": [
          "def-td-si-quantities"
        ],
        "justified_by": [],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-td-si-quantities": "formulation-prerequisite"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Let $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be an open interval measured in seconds, let $L_1,L_2,L_3>0$ be lengths in metres, and set $\\Omega=\\prod_{i=1}^3(0,L_i)\\subseteq\\mathbb R^3$ with closure $\\overline\\Omega$. A rectangular local-equilibrium field record consists of an open temperature interval $\\mathcal T\\subset(0,\\infty)$, a temperature field $T:\\overline\\Omega\\times I\\to\\mathcal T$ whose time derivative through order one and spatial derivatives through order two extend continuously to $\\overline\\Omega\\times I$, a function $u\\in C^1(\\mathcal T,\\mathbb R)$ of internal-energy density in $\\mathrm{J\\,m^{-3}}$, and its volumetric heat capacity $c(T):=u'(T)>0$ in $\\mathrm{J\\,m^{-3}\\,K^{-1}}$. The coordinates $x_i$ are lengths in metres, and $T$ is measured in kelvins, using [[def-td-si-quantities]].\n\nThe record may also include a heat-flux field $j_Q:\\overline\\Omega\\times I\\to\\mathbb R^3$ with components in $\\mathrm{W\\,m^{-2}}$ and a conductivity field $K:\\overline\\Omega\\times\\mathcal T\\to\\mathbb R^{3\\times3}$ with entries in $\\mathrm{W\\,m^{-1}\\,K^{-1}}$. The flux and tensor are field data here; no relation between them is imposed by this Definition. In Euclidean coordinates, $\\nabla T=(\\partial_{x_1}T,\\partial_{x_2}T,\\partial_{x_3}T)$ and $\\operatorname{div}j_Q=\\sum_i\\partial_{x_i}(j_Q)_i$. On a flat face, $n$ denotes its outward unit normal.\n\nThis is a specified regular field class on a fixed rigid body. It does not assert that every physical medium has local-equilibrium fields, nor does it adopt a heat equation, a boundary condition, Fourier's law, or an entropy-production law.",
        "strategy": "Definition carrier is in items/def-td-rectangular-local-equilibrium-fields.md.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Konstantin Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics: Statistical Mechanics, §6.5, heat conduction",
              "url": "https://batch.libretexts.org/print/Letter/Finished/phys-32579/Full.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "MIT OpenCourseWare, 18.303 Linear Partial Differential Equations, The 1-D Heat Equation",
              "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-303-linear-partial-differential-equations-fall-2006/d11b374a85c3fde55ec971fe587f8a50_heateqni.pdf"
            }
          ]
        },
        "library": "physics"
      },
      {
        "id": "def-td-regular-elastic-collision-model",
        "kind": "definition",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "Regular single-species elastic collision data",
        "reference": "items/def-td-regular-elastic-collision-model.md#definition",
        "design_state": "Complete draft carrier authored; scoped checks and independent review remain pending.",
        "dependency_level": 1,
        "deps": [
          "def-td-si-quantities",
          "def-tdmath-elastic-collision-kernel"
        ],
        "justified_by": [],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-td-si-quantities": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "def-tdmath-elastic-collision-kernel": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Fix a particle mass $m_p>0$ in kilograms and velocity space $V=\\mathbb R^3$ with components in $\\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$. The elastic collision set is\n$$Q_{\\rm el}=\\{(v,w,v',w')\\in V^4:v+w=v'+w',\\ |v|^2+|w|^2=|v'|^2+|w'|^2\\}.$$\nThus each allowed event conserves momentum $m_p(v+w)$ and kinetic energy $m_p(|v|^2+|w|^2)/2$. Equip $Q_{\\rm el}$ with the Borel sets generated by its relative topology.\n\nCollision-rate data consist of a nonnegative, countably additive, $\\sigma$-finite Borel measure $\\nu$ on these Borel sets. It is invariant under exchanging $v$ with $w$, exchanging $v'$ with $w'$, and exchanging the incoming pair $(v,w)$ with the outgoing pair $(v',w')$: for each of these maps $S$ and Borel set $E$, $\\nu(S^{-1}E)=\\nu(E)$. This is the three-dimensional physical restriction of the collision set and exchange conventions in [[def-tdmath-elastic-collision-kernel]], with $\\sigma$-finiteness stated explicitly for the product integrals used by the entropy theorem. Its physical units and rate normalization are chosen so the associated weak collision operator has units of density per second. The measure is a declared model input; no differential cross section or microscopic limit is inferred from this Definition.",
        "strategy": "Definition carrier is in items/def-td-regular-elastic-collision-model.md.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Daniel Arovas, Lecture Notes on Thermodynamics and Statistical Mechanics, §8.10",
              "url": "https://courses.physics.ucsd.edu/2010/Spring/physics210a/LECTURES/STATMECH.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "Konstantin Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics: Statistical Mechanics, Chapter 6",
              "url": "https://batch.libretexts.org/print/Letter/Finished/phys-32579/Full.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "R. Fitzpatrick, Plasma Physics, Boltzmann H-Theorem",
              "url": "https://farside.ph.utexas.edu/teaching/plasma/lectures1/node40.html"
            }
          ]
        },
        "library": "physics"
      },
      {
        "id": "post-td-fourier-conductivity",
        "kind": "postulate",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "Fourier heat-flux constitutive model",
        "reference": "items/post-td-fourier-conductivity.md#postulate",
        "design_state": "Complete draft carrier authored; scoped checks and independent review remain pending.",
        "dependency_level": 2,
        "deps": [
          "def-td-rectangular-local-equilibrium-fields"
        ],
        "justified_by": [],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-td-rectangular-local-equilibrium-fields": "formulation-prerequisite"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "For the field record of [[def-td-rectangular-local-equilibrium-fields]], adopt\n$$j_Q(x,t)=-K(x,T(x,t))\\nabla T(x,t).$$\nHere $K$ has entries in $\\mathrm{W\\,m^{-1}\\,K^{-1}}$ and $j_Q$ in $\\mathrm{W\\,m^{-2}}$, so the equation is dimensionally consistent. Write $K_s=(K+K^{\\mathsf T})/2$ for the symmetric part and assume $z^{\\mathsf T}K_s(x,T)z\\ge0$ for every $z\\in\\mathbb R^3$. The antisymmetric part may contribute to the flux but not to the quadratic entropy production.\n\nThis is an adopted constitutive model in the stated continuum regime. It is neither a consequence of equilibrium entropy alone nor a universal law for all media. In particular, it does not assert a finite propagation speed or an empirical value for $K$.",
        "strategy": "Adopted physical postulate; no theorem proof is claimed.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Konstantin Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics: Statistical Mechanics, §6.5, heat conduction",
              "url": "https://batch.libretexts.org/print/Letter/Finished/phys-32579/Full.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "MIT OpenCourseWare, 18.303 Linear Partial Differential Equations, The 1-D Heat Equation",
              "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-303-linear-partial-differential-equations-fall-2006/d11b374a85c3fde55ec971fe587f8a50_heateqni.pdf"
            }
          ]
        },
        "library": "physics"
      },
      {
        "id": "post-td-local-energy-balance",
        "kind": "postulate",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "Local energy balance for an insulated continuum body",
        "reference": "items/post-td-local-energy-balance.md#postulate",
        "design_state": "Complete draft carrier authored; scoped checks and independent review remain pending.",
        "dependency_level": 5,
        "deps": [
          "def-td-rectangular-local-equilibrium-fields",
          "post-td-first-law-energy"
        ],
        "justified_by": [],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-td-rectangular-local-equilibrium-fields": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "post-td-first-law-energy": "formulation-prerequisite"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "For a field record on $\\Omega\\times I$ from [[def-td-rectangular-local-equilibrium-fields]], adopt the local balance\n$$\\partial_t u(T(x,t))+\\operatorname{div}j_Q(x,t)=0$$\nand the insulated-boundary condition $j_Q(x,t)\\cdot n(x)=0$ on every face of $\\partial\\Omega$. Both terms in the local equation have units $\\mathrm{W\\,m^{-3}}$. The boundary condition says that no heat energy crosses the body's outer faces in this model.\n\nThis local continuum balance is an additional adopted postulate, with the sign and energy convention of [[post-td-first-law-energy]]. It is not deduced from that global process-record balance. Matter transfer, volume work, internal sources, moving boundaries, and external-field energy require separately specified balance models and are outside this item.",
        "strategy": "Adopted physical postulate; no theorem proof is claimed.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Konstantin Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics: Statistical Mechanics, §6.5, heat conduction",
              "url": "https://batch.libretexts.org/print/Letter/Finished/phys-32579/Full.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "MIT OpenCourseWare, 18.303 Linear Partial Differential Equations, The 1-D Heat Equation",
              "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-303-linear-partial-differential-equations-fall-2006/d11b374a85c3fde55ec971fe587f8a50_heateqni.pdf"
            }
          ]
        },
        "library": "physics"
      },
      {
        "id": "post-td-local-equilibrium-entropy-density",
        "kind": "postulate",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "Local-equilibrium caloric entropy density",
        "reference": "items/post-td-local-equilibrium-entropy-density.md#postulate",
        "design_state": "Complete draft carrier authored; scoped checks and independent review remain pending.",
        "dependency_level": 5,
        "deps": [
          "def-td-rectangular-local-equilibrium-fields",
          "post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy"
        ],
        "justified_by": [],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-td-rectangular-local-equilibrium-fields": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy": "formulation-prerequisite"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": true,
        "statement": "For the local-equilibrium medium of [[def-td-rectangular-local-equilibrium-fields]], adopt a local entropy density $s(T)$ measured in $\\mathrm{J\\,m^{-3}\\,K^{-1}}$ with\n$$\\frac{ds}{dT}(T)=\\frac{c(T)}{T},$$\nwhere $c(T)=u'(T)>0$ is the volumetric heat capacity and $T>0$ is measured in kelvins. Equivalently, for a fixed reference $T_{\\rm ref}>0$ and reference density $s_{\\rm ref}$, set\n$$s(T)=s_{\\rm ref}+\\int_{T_{\\rm ref}}^T\\frac{c(\\theta)}{\\theta}\\,d\\theta.$$\nThe volume integral of this field is the entropy assigned by this continuum model. This adopts a local thermodynamic relation extending the macroscopic entropy framework of [[post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy]]; it does not prove that every evolving medium has such a local entropy density or identify it with an arbitrary finite-system entropy function.\n\nFor constant $c(T)=c_0>0$, the adopted relation gives $s(T)=s_{\\rm ref}+c_0\\log(T/T_{\\rm ref})$. This is a restricted constitutive specialization, not a universal equation of state.",
        "strategy": "Adopted physical postulate; no theorem proof is claimed.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Konstantin Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics: Statistical Mechanics, §§1.3 and 6.5",
              "url": "https://batch.libretexts.org/print/Letter/Finished/phys-32579/Full.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "Daniel Arovas, Lecture Notes on Thermodynamics and Statistical Mechanics",
              "url": "https://courses.physics.ucsd.edu/2010/Spring/physics210a/LECTURES/STATMECH.pdf"
            }
          ]
        },
        "library": "physics"
      },
      {
        "id": "post-td-boltzmann-molecular-chaos-model",
        "kind": "postulate",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "Periodic Boltzmann model with incoming molecular chaos",
        "reference": "items/post-td-boltzmann-molecular-chaos-model.md#postulate",
        "design_state": "Complete draft carrier authored; scoped checks and independent review remain pending.",
        "dependency_level": 2,
        "deps": [
          "def-td-regular-elastic-collision-model",
          "def-td-si-quantities"
        ],
        "justified_by": [],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-td-regular-elastic-collision-model": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "def-td-si-quantities": "formulation-prerequisite"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Let $\\Omega_{\\rm per}=\\prod_{i=1}^3(0,L_i)$ with opposite faces identified, let $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be an open time interval in seconds, and let $f(x,v,t)\\ge0$ be a one-particle number density for $x\\in\\Omega_{\\rm per}$, $v\\in\\mathbb R^3$, and $t\\in I$. With $x$ in metres and $v$ in $\\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$, $f$ has units $\\mathrm{s^3\\,m^{-6}}$ relative to $d^3x\\,d^3v$; its moments used below are assumed finite. Periodic boundary conditions are imposed in $x$.\n\nAdopt the incoming molecular-chaos closure: at a collision location and time, the incoming two-particle velocity density used in the collision term is $f(x,v,t)f(x,w,t)$. This is a one-sided factorization assumption; no factorization of the outgoing pair and no derivation from reversible many-particle mechanics is asserted.\n\nFor the elastic data $Q_{\\rm el},\\nu$ of [[def-td-regular-elastic-collision-model]], adopt a collision operator $Q_\\nu(f)$, with units of $f$ per second, satisfying for every measurable test function $\\varphi$ for which the integrals converge absolutely,\n$$\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\varphi(v)Q_\\nu(f)(x,v,t)\\,d^3v=\\frac14\\int_{Q_{\\rm el}}\\big(f(x,v',t)f(x,w',t)-f(x,v,t)f(x,w,t)\\big)\\big(\\varphi(v)+\\varphi(w)-\\varphi(v')-\\varphi(w')\\big)\\,d\\nu.$$\nThe adopted kinetic equation is\n$$\\partial_t f+v\\cdot\\nabla_x f=Q_\\nu(f).$$\nThe equation and weak collision identity are model postulates. This item does not assert a hard-sphere Boltzmann–Grad limit, propagation of chaos, or universal equilibration.",
        "strategy": "Adopted physical postulate; no theorem proof is claimed.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Daniel Arovas, Lecture Notes on Thermodynamics and Statistical Mechanics, §8.10",
              "url": "https://courses.physics.ucsd.edu/2010/Spring/physics210a/LECTURES/STATMECH.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "Konstantin Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics: Statistical Mechanics, Chapter 6",
              "url": "https://batch.libretexts.org/print/Letter/Finished/phys-32579/Full.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "MIT OpenCourseWare, 8.333 Statistical Mechanics I, Lecture 9",
              "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-333-statistical-mechanics-i-statistical-mechanics-of-particles-fall-2013/ce250b44390106351cb97aafb0060ca7_MIT8_333F13_Lec9.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "R. Fitzpatrick, Plasma Physics, Boltzmann H-Theorem",
              "url": "https://farside.ph.utexas.edu/teaching/plasma/lectures1/node40.html"
            }
          ]
        },
        "library": "physics"
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-td-rectangular-poincare",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Mean-zero Poincare inequality on a rectangle",
        "reference": "items/lem-td-rectangular-poincare.md#statement",
        "design_state": "Complete draft carrier authored; scoped checks and independent review remain pending.",
        "dependency_level": 0,
        "deps": [
          "thm-newton-leibniz-with-interior-derivative",
          "thm-riemann-fubini-on-product-rectangles"
        ],
        "justified_by": [],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "thm-newton-leibniz-with-interior-derivative": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-riemann-fubini-on-product-rectangles": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": true,
        "statement": "Let $d\\ge1$, let $L_1,\\ldots,L_d>0$, and let $\\Omega=\\prod_{i=1}^d(0,L_i)$. If $w\\in C^1(\\overline\\Omega,\\mathbb R)$ has zero mean, then\n$$\\int_\\Omega |w(x)|^2\\,dx\\le \\frac d2\\sum_{i=1}^d L_i^2\\int_\\Omega |\\partial_iw(x)|^2\\,dx\\le C_\\Omega\\int_\\Omega |\\nabla w(x)|^2\\,dx,$$\nwhere $C_\\Omega=\\frac d2\\max_iL_i^2$. All integrals are the multidimensional Riemann integrals of continuous functions on the indicated closed rectangles, with the usual extension to their interiors.",
        "strategy": "proof_strategy: direct; complete argument is in items/lem-td-rectangular-poincare.md and its proof contract.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "John K. Hunter, Notes on Partial Differential Equations, §4.4",
              "url": "https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/pdes/ch4.pdf"
            }
          ]
        },
        "library": "mathematics"
      },
      {
        "id": "pthm-td-collision-h-theorem",
        "kind": "physics-theorem",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "Conditional entropy production in the periodic Boltzmann model",
        "reference": "items/pthm-td-collision-h-theorem.md#statement",
        "design_state": "Complete draft carrier authored; scoped checks and independent review remain pending.",
        "dependency_level": 3,
        "deps": [
          "def-td-regular-elastic-collision-model",
          "def-td-si-quantities",
          "post-td-boltzmann-molecular-chaos-model",
          "thm-tdmath-regular-collision-entropy"
        ],
        "justified_by": [],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-td-regular-elastic-collision-model": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "def-td-si-quantities": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "post-td-boltzmann-molecular-chaos-model": "physical-assumption",
          "thm-tdmath-regular-collision-entropy": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Let $\\Omega_{\\rm per}=\\prod_{i=1}^3(0,L_i)$ with opposite faces identified, and let $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be an open time interval. Assume the current conditional mathematical result [[thm-tdmath-regular-collision-entropy]] at its exact draft interface [L1] below. Let $f:\\Omega_{\\rm per}\\times\\mathbb R^3\\times I\\to(0,\\infty)$ satisfy the periodic Boltzmann postulate [[post-td-boltzmann-molecular-chaos-model]] and the regularity, integrability, and domination hypotheses in [A2]–[A3]. Fix a positive constant reference density $f_{\\rm ref}$ with the same units as $f$, and define the kinetic entropy\n$$S_{\\rm kin}(t)=-k_B\\int_{\\Omega_{\\rm per}}\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}f(x,v,t)\\log\\!\\left(\\frac{f(x,v,t)}{f_{\\rm ref}}\\right)\\,d^3v\\,d^3x.$$\nThen\n$$\\frac{dS_{\\rm kin}}{dt}=\\frac{k_B}{4}\\int_{\\Omega_{\\rm per}}\\int_{Q_{\\rm el}}(A-B)\\log\\!\\left(\\frac AB\\right)\\,d\\nu\\,d^3x\\ge0,$$\nwhere $A=f(x,v,t)f(x,w,t)$ and $B=f(x,v',t)f(x,w',t)$; the supplier's $\\log A-\\log B$ denotes the dimensionless difference $\\log(A/B)$ for these positive quantities of equal units. Equality holds exactly when $A=B$ for $\\nu$-almost every collision at almost every spatial point. This is a conditional deduction inside the adopted incoming-molecular-chaos kinetic model. The cited mathematical carrier is currently draft and has not received independent proof acceptance; no microscopic derivation, experimental claim, or Maxwellian characterization is included.",
        "strategy": "proof_strategy: direct; complete argument is in items/pthm-td-collision-h-theorem.md and its proof contract.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Daniel Arovas, Lecture Notes on Thermodynamics and Statistical Mechanics, §8.10",
              "url": "https://courses.physics.ucsd.edu/2010/Spring/physics210a/LECTURES/STATMECH.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "Konstantin Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics: Statistical Mechanics, Chapter 6",
              "url": "https://batch.libretexts.org/print/Letter/Finished/phys-32579/Full.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "MIT OpenCourseWare, 8.333 Statistical Mechanics I, Lecture 9",
              "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-333-statistical-mechanics-i-statistical-mechanics-of-particles-fall-2013/ce250b44390106351cb97aafb0060ca7_MIT8_333F13_Lec9.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "R. Fitzpatrick, Plasma Physics, Boltzmann H-Theorem",
              "url": "https://farside.ph.utexas.edu/teaching/plasma/lectures1/node40.html"
            }
          ]
        },
        "library": "physics"
      },
      {
        "id": "pthm-td-fourier-entropy-production",
        "kind": "physics-theorem",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "Entropy production in the local Fourier heat model",
        "reference": "items/pthm-td-fourier-entropy-production.md#statement",
        "design_state": "Complete draft carrier authored; scoped checks and independent review remain pending.",
        "dependency_level": 6,
        "deps": [
          "def-td-rectangular-local-equilibrium-fields",
          "post-td-local-energy-balance",
          "post-td-fourier-conductivity",
          "post-td-local-equilibrium-entropy-density",
          "thm-newton-leibniz-with-interior-derivative",
          "thm-riemann-fubini-on-product-rectangles"
        ],
        "justified_by": [],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-td-rectangular-local-equilibrium-fields": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "post-td-local-energy-balance": "physical-assumption",
          "post-td-fourier-conductivity": "physical-assumption",
          "post-td-local-equilibrium-entropy-density": "physical-assumption",
          "thm-newton-leibniz-with-interior-derivative": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-riemann-fubini-on-product-rectangles": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Let $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be an open time interval, $\\Omega=\\prod_{i=1}^3(0,L_i)$ a fixed rectangle, and $(T,u,j_Q,K)$ a field record from [[def-td-rectangular-local-equilibrium-fields]] satisfying the adopted local-energy, Fourier, and local-entropy postulates [[post-td-local-energy-balance]], [[post-td-fourier-conductivity]], and [[post-td-local-equilibrium-entropy-density]]. Assume additionally that $j_Q$ and its first spatial derivatives are continuous on every compact subset of $\\overline\\Omega\\times I$. Then, throughout $\\Omega\\times I$,\n$$\\partial_t s(T)+\\operatorname{div}\\!\\left(\\frac{j_Q}{T}\\right)=\\frac{(\\nabla T)^{\\mathsf T}K_s(x,T)\\nabla T}{T^2}\\ge0,$$\nwhere $K_s=(K+K^{\\mathsf T})/2$. For every compact time interval $[t_0,t_1]\\subset I$, the model's total entropy $S(t)=\\int_\\Omega s(T(x,t))\\,dx$ is differentiable and nondecreasing, while $\\int_\\Omega u(T(x,t))\\,dx$ is constant. These are conditional deductions of the declared continuum model, not observations or a claim that every medium obeys Fourier's law.",
        "strategy": "proof_strategy: direct; complete argument is in items/pthm-td-fourier-entropy-production.md and its proof contract.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
