          "thm-heat-cauchy-solution-for-bounded-continuous-data": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-newton-leibniz-with-interior-derivative": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $\\Omega=\\prod_{i=1}^3(0,L_i)$ with $L_i>0$ metres, let $c>0$ be a constant volumetric heat capacity in $\\mathrm{J\\,m^{-3}\\,K^{-1}}$ with $u'(T)=c$ throughout the range of $g$, let $k>0$ be a constant scalar conductivity in $\\mathrm{W\\,m^{-1}\\,K^{-1}}$ with $K\\equiv kI_3$ in [[post-td-fourier-conductivity]], and put $a=k/c$ in $\\mathrm{m^2\\,s^{-1}}$.\n\nFor $t>0$ define\n$$T(x,t)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\Gamma(x-y,at)g(y)\\,dy,\\qquad \\Gamma(z,\\theta)=(4\\pi\\theta)^{-3/2}e^{-|z|^2/(4\\theta)},$$\nand set $T(x,0)=g(x)$ on $\\overline\\Omega$. This field is the unique $C^1$-in-time, $C^2$-in-space solution on each finite time interval of $c\\partial_tT=k\\Delta T$ on $\\Omega$, with $\\partial_nT=0$ on every face and the given initial value. It satisfies $T_{\\min}\\le T(x,t)\\le T_{\\max}$ for all $t\\ge0$, has $C^2$ spatial derivatives continuous to $t=0$, and its right time derivative there is $a\\Delta g$. Under [[post-td-local-energy-balance]] and [[post-td-fourier-conductivity]], it is a global-in-time solution of that restricted physical continuum model.",
        "strategy": "proof_strategy: direct; complete argument is in items/pthm-td-constant-conductivity-neumann-existence.md and its proof contract.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "John K. Hunter, Notes on Partial Differential Equations, §5.1",
              "url": "https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/pdes/ch5.pdf"
            }
          ]
        },
        "library": "physics"
      },
      {
        "id": "pthm-td-neumann-heat-relaxation",
        "kind": "physics-theorem",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "Exponential relaxation of the constant-conductivity Neumann heat model",
        "reference": "items/pthm-td-neumann-heat-relaxation.md#statement",
        "design_state": "Complete draft carrier authored; scoped checks and independent review remain pending.",
        "dependency_level": 7,
        "deps": [
          "pthm-td-constant-conductivity-neumann-existence",
          "def-countable-choice",
          "lem-td-rectangular-poincare",
          "thm-newton-leibniz-with-interior-derivative",
          "thm-riemann-fubini-on-product-rectangles"
        ],
        "justified_by": [],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "pthm-td-constant-conductivity-neumann-existence": "physical-result",
          "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-td-rectangular-poincare": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-newton-leibniz-with-interior-derivative": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-riemann-fubini-on-product-rectangles": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Assume Countable Choice and the hypotheses and solution $T$ of [[pthm-td-constant-conductivity-neumann-existence]], let $V=|\\Omega|=L_1L_2L_3$, define the spatial mean $\\overline T(t)=V^{-1}\\int_\\Omega T(x,t)\\,dx$, and put $E(t)=\\int_\\Omega|T(x,t)-\\overline T(t)|^2\\,dx$. With $a=k/c$ and $C_\\Omega=\\frac32\\max_iL_i^2$, the mean is constant and\n$$E(t)\\le e^{-2at/C_\\Omega}E(0)\\qquad(t\\ge0).$$\nThus this model solution converges exponentially to its initial spatial mean in $L^2(\\Omega)$; no pointwise-uniform convergence rate is asserted.",
        "strategy": "proof_strategy: direct; complete argument is in items/pthm-td-neumann-heat-relaxation.md and its proof contract.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "John K. Hunter, Notes on Partial Differential Equations, §4.4 and §5.1",
              "url": "https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/pdes/ch4.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "John K. Hunter, Notes on Partial Differential Equations, §5.1",
              "url": "https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/pdes/ch5.pdf"
            }
          ]
        },
        "library": "physics"
      },
      {
        "id": "rem-td-solution-existence-scope",
        "kind": "remark",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "Scope of the continuum heat solution results",
        "reference": "items/rem-td-solution-existence-scope.md#remarks",
        "design_state": "Complete draft carrier authored; scoped checks and independent review remain pending.",
        "dependency_level": 8,
        "deps": [
          "pthm-td-constant-conductivity-neumann-existence",
          "pthm-td-neumann-heat-relaxation"
        ],
        "justified_by": [],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "pthm-td-constant-conductivity-neumann-existence": "physical-result",
          "pthm-td-neumann-heat-relaxation": "physical-result"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "The existence and relaxation results on this page apply to a fixed rectangle, constant positive $c$ and $k$, homogeneous Neumann boundary data, and the specified even-periodic $C^2$ initial profiles. They do not prove existence for rough initial data, arbitrary domains or boundary conditions, state-dependent conductivity, or a microscopic Boltzmann-to-Fourier limit. The Fourier heat equation is a parabolic model: its Gaussian kernel gives an immediate nonzero response throughout the rectangle for the compactly supported perturbation in [[ex-td-diffusive-infinite-propagation]]. That is a mathematical property of this adopted diffusion model; it is not a measurement or a claim about finite-speed physical signals.",
        "strategy": "Source-backed scope note; no proof claim is made.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Konstantin Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics: Statistical Mechanics, §6.5, heat conduction",
              "url": "https://batch.libretexts.org/print/Letter/Finished/phys-32579/Full.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "John K. Hunter, Notes on Partial Differential Equations, Chapter 5",
              "url": "https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/pdes/ch5.pdf"
            }
          ]
        },
        "library": "physics"
      }
    ]
