      {
        "id": "ex-td-insulated-polynomial-relaxation",
        "kind": "example",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "A nonuniform insulated temperature profile relaxes to its mean",
        "reference": "items/ex-td-insulated-polynomial-relaxation.md#statement",
        "design_state": "Complete draft carrier authored; scoped checks and independent review remain pending.",
        "dependency_level": 8,
        "deps": [
          "pthm-td-constant-conductivity-neumann-existence",
          "pthm-td-neumann-heat-relaxation",
          "def-countable-choice"
        ],
        "justified_by": [],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "pthm-td-constant-conductivity-neumann-existence": "physical-result",
          "pthm-td-neumann-heat-relaxation": "physical-result",
          "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Assume Countable Choice as in [[pthm-td-constant-conductivity-neumann-existence]]. Let $\\Omega=\\prod_{i=1}^3(0,L_i)$, let $c,k>0$, and let $T_b>\\epsilon/30>0$, where $T_b$ and $\\epsilon$ are in kelvins. For $r\\in[0,1]$ set $h(r)=r^2(1-r)^2-1/30$ and prescribe\n$$g(x)=T_b+\\epsilon h(x_1/L_1)\\qquad(x\\in\\overline\\Omega).$$\nUse its even $2L_i$-periodic extension in each coordinate as the initial datum on $\\mathbb R^3$. The unique insulated heat solution has constant spatial mean $T_b$ and satisfies\n$$\\int_\\Omega|T(x,t)-T_b|^2\\,dx\\le \\frac{\\epsilon^2|\\Omega|}{2100}e^{-2(k/c)t/C_\\Omega},\\qquad C_\\Omega=\\frac32\\max_iL_i^2.$$\nThis gives an exactly computed initial variance and the conditional relaxation estimate of the model.",
        "strategy": "proof_strategy: direct; complete argument is in items/ex-td-insulated-polynomial-relaxation.md and its proof contract.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-generated",
          "proof": "ai-generated"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Konstantin Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics: Statistical Mechanics, §6.5, heat conduction",
              "url": "https://batch.libretexts.org/print/Letter/Finished/phys-32579/Full.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "MIT OpenCourseWare, 18.303 Linear Partial Differential Equations, The 1-D Heat Equation",
              "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-303-linear-partial-differential-equations-fall-2006/d11b374a85c3fde55ec971fe587f8a50_heateqni.pdf"
            }
          ]
        },
        "library": "physics"
      },
      {
        "id": "ex-td-diffusive-infinite-propagation",
        "kind": "example",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "The rectangular Fourier model has instantaneous spatial support",
        "reference": "items/ex-td-diffusive-infinite-propagation.md#statement",
        "design_state": "Complete draft carrier authored; scoped checks and independent review remain pending.",
        "dependency_level": 7,
        "deps": [
          "pthm-td-constant-conductivity-neumann-existence",
          "def-countable-choice"
        ],
        "justified_by": [],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "pthm-td-constant-conductivity-neumann-existence": "physical-result",
          "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Assume Countable Choice as in [[pthm-td-constant-conductivity-neumann-existence]]. Let $\\Omega=\\prod_{i=1}^3(0,L_i)$, choose $x_c=(L_1/2,L_2/2,L_3/2)$ and $0<r<\\frac12\\min_iL_i$, and define on $\\overline\\Omega$\n$$\\phi(x)=\\begin{cases}(1-|x-x_c|^2/r^2)^3,&|x-x_c|<r,\\\\0,&|x-x_c|\\ge r.\\end{cases}$$\nFor $L>0$, define the even periodic fold $\\rho_L:\\mathbb R\\to[0,L]$ by $\\rho_L(s)=s-2nL$ for $2nL\\le s\\le(2n+1)L$ and $\\rho_L(s)=(2n+2)L-s$ for $(2n+1)L\\le s\\le(2n+2)L$, $n\\in\\mathbb Z$. Put $\\phi_{\\rm per}(x)=\\phi(\\rho_{L_1}(x_1),\\rho_{L_2}(x_2),\\rho_{L_3}(x_3))$ and take $g=T_b+\\epsilon\\phi_{\\rm per}$ with $T_b,\\epsilon>0$. In the constant-$c,k$ insulated Fourier model, the heat-kernel solution satisfies\n$$T(x,t)-T_b=\\epsilon\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\Gamma(x-y,(k/c)t)\\phi_{\\rm per}(y)\\,dy>0$$\nfor every interior $x\\in\\Omega$ and every $t>0$, although the initial perturbation is supported in the compact ball $\\overline B(x_c,r)\\subset\\Omega$. Thus the adopted diffusion model has no finite propagation speed for this temperature perturbation.",
        "strategy": "proof_strategy: direct; complete argument is in items/ex-td-diffusive-infinite-propagation.md and its proof contract.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-generated",
          "proof": "ai-generated"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Konstantin Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics: Statistical Mechanics, §6.5, heat conduction",
              "url": "https://batch.libretexts.org/print/Letter/Finished/phys-32579/Full.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "John K. Hunter, Notes on Partial Differential Equations, §5.1",
              "url": "https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/pdes/ch5.pdf"
            }
          ]
        },
        "library": "physics"
